===== Weak Verbs =====

In Ugaritic, “weak verbs” are verbs whose roots contain a weak consonant, that is, a consonant that may disappear in some forms (in particular the imperative), or change into another consonant (some imperfect forms). Weak consonants are ''w'' and ''y'', and also ''n'' if it is the first root consonant. Verbs with only two root consonants are weak too. 

Due to their weak consonants, weak verbs can undergo phonetic changes, such as the assimilation of waw (''w'') to yod (''y''), especially in the absence of an intervening vowel. This characteristic impacts the verb's inflection, resulting in variations that are atypical compared to regular (strong) verbs.<ref name="Gordon 1998 p. ">{{cite book | last=Gordon | first=Cyrus Herzl | title=Ugaritic Textbook | publisher=Gregorian Biblical BookShop | publication-place=Roma | date=1998 | isbn=88-7653-238-2 | page=13}}</ref> This phenomenon is akin to that observed in other Semitic languages, including Hebrew.

