=====Case=====
Ugaritic has three [[grammatical cases]] corresponding to: [[nominative]], [[genitive]], and [[accusative]]. Normally, [[singulative number|singular]] [[nouns]] take the ending&nbsp;''-u'' in the [[nominative]], ''-i'' in the [[genitive]] and&nbsp;''-a'' in the [[accusative]]. After prepositions as a rule the genitive is used. The accusative is also used [[adverb]]ially (''ṭābu'', “good” > ''ṭāba'', “well”) and as a kind of [[locative]] (''šamîma'' = “to the heavens, in heaven”). More often, a locative is formed by appending a suffix ''-h'' to the accusative: ''’arṣu'', “earth”, accusative ''’arṣa'', locative ''’arṣah'', “earthward”. There is no [[dative]]; instead the preposition ''lê'', “to, for”, + genitive is used.

As in [[Arabic language|Arabic]], some exceptional nouns and personal names (known as [[diptotes]]) have the [[suffix]] ''-a'' in the [[genitive]], thus making it equal to the accusative. There is no Ugaritic equivalent for Classical Arabic [[nunation]] or Akkadian mimation.

=====State=====
[[Nouns]] in Ugaritic occur in two [[compound (linguistics)|states]]: [[absolute state|absolute]] and [[status constructus|construct]].
The construct (or ‘bound’) state indicates that a noun is closely linked to the following noun. For example, “the house of the king” could in Ugaritic in principle be expressed in two ways:

1. “the house” (absolute state) “<u>of</u> the king” (absolute state, genitive). This might be called the ‘Latin’ way of expression (''domus regis'');

2. “the house <u>of</u>” (construct state) “the king” (absolute state, genitive). This might be called the ‘Hebrew’ way of expression (''bēt hammelek'').

The construct state is also the basic form used when a personal pronoun is suffixed: ''malakūma'' = “(the) kings” (absolute state, nominative) > ''malakū'' (construct state) > ''malakūhu'' = “his kings”; similarly ''malakĩhu'' = “(of) his kings” (genitive, accusative).

Ugaritic, unlike [[Arabic]] and [[Hebrew]], has no [[definiteness|definite]] [[article (grammar)|article]].

=====Gender=====
[[Nouns]] which have no [[gender]] [[marker (linguistics)|marker]] are for the most part masculine, although some feminine [[nouns]] do not have a feminine [[marker (linguistics)|marker]]. However, these denote feminine beings such as ʼumm- (mother).  /-t/ is the feminine marker which is directly attached to the base of the noun.

=====Number=====
Ugaritic distinguishes between [[nouns]] based on quantity. All [[nouns]] are either [[singulative number|singular]] when there is one, [[dual (grammatical number)|dual]] when there are two, and [[plural]] if there are three or more.

* The '''[[singulative number|singular]]''' has no [[marker (linguistics)|marker]] and is [[inflected]] according to its [[grammatical cases|case]] (''malku'', “king”: genitive ''malki'', accusative ''malka'').

* The marker for the '''[[dual (grammatical number)|dual]]''' in the [[absolute state]] appears as /-m/. However, the [[Speech production|vocalization]] may be reconstructed as /-āma/ or /-āmi/ in the [[nominative]] (such as ''malkāma, malkāmi'' "two kings") and /-êma/ or /-êmi/ for the [[genitive]] and [[accusative]] (e.g. ''malkêma, malkêmi''). For the [[status constructus|construct state]], it is /-ā/ and /-ê/ respectively.

* Masculine [[absolute state]] '''[[plurals]]''' take the forms ''-ūma'' in the [[nominative]] and ''-īma'' in the [[genitive]] and [[accusative]]. In the [[status constructus|construct state]] they are ''-ū'' and ''-ī'' respectively. There are a few irregular (or [[broken plurals|broken]]) plurals; for example ''bt'' (''bêtu''), “house”, plural ''bhtm'' (''bahatūma''); and ''bn'' (''binu''), “son”, plural ''banūma'' (with [[Ablaut]]).

: The female afformative [[plural]] is /-āt/ with a [[grammatical cases|case]] [[marker (linguistics)|marker]] probably following the /-t/, giving /-ātu/ for the [[nominative]] and /-āti/ for the [[genitive]] and [[accusative]] in both [[absolute state|absolute]] and [[status constructus|construct state]].

=====Adjectives=====
[[Adjectives]] follow the [[noun]] and are [[declension|declined]] exactly like the preceding [[noun]].

